\title{The Complete Extensions of an Argumentation Framework Form a Meet-Semilattice}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-14}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the order-theoretic structure of the \emph{complete extensions} of an
abstract argumentation framework in the sense of Dung. Working with an arbitrary
attack relation $R$ on an arbitrary set $A$ of arguments---imposing no finiteness,
acyclicity, or well-foundedness hypothesis---we prove that the complete
extensions, ordered by inclusion, form a \emph{meet-semilattice}: every nonempty
family of complete extensions possesses a greatest lower bound that is again a
complete extension. The engine of the construction is a single lemma: the defense
(characteristic) operator $F$ maps the intersection of any family of complete
extensions into itself. Consequently $F$ restricts to a monotone self-map of the
interval below that intersection, and its greatest fixed point there---built
explicitly as a Knaster--Tarski union of post-fixed points---is the desired meet.
Specializing to the family of \emph{all} complete extensions recovers the
\emph{grounded extension} as the bottom element of the semilattice, re-deriving
its existence and uniqueness purely order-theoretically, without transfinite
iteration. We include self-contained proofs of Dung's Fundamental Lemma and of the
unconditional existence of a complete extension via Zorn's Lemma.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Abstract argumentation, introduced by Dung, models defeasible reasoning by
abstracting away the internal content of arguments and retaining only a binary
\emph{attack} relation between them. Formally, an \emph{argumentation framework}
is a pair $(A, R)$ where $A$ is a set of arguments and $R \subseteq A \times A$ is
the attack relation; we write $R\,a\,b$ for ``$a$ attacks $b$''. Over this spare
structure, Dung defined a hierarchy of \emph{extensions}---sets of arguments that
can be rationally accepted together---including admissible, complete, grounded,
preferred, and stable extensions. These semantics underpin nonmonotonic
reasoning, legal and medical decision support, multi-agent systems, and the
reconciliation of inconsistent knowledge bases.

The complete extensions are known to form a complete lattice under favorable
finiteness conditions. The present work isolates the \emph{meet} half of that
structure and establishes it in full generality: for an arbitrary attack relation
on an arbitrary carrier set, every nonempty family of complete extensions has a
greatest lower bound that is itself complete. No finiteness, well-foundedness, or
acyclicity is required.

Our contributions are: (1) a short self-contained development of the basic Dung
semantics (Section~\ref{sec:defs}); (2) the decisive lemma that $F$ maps the
intersection of complete extensions into itself (Theorem~\ref{thm:decisive}); (3)
an explicit meet construction via a Knaster--Tarski union, with the
meet-semilattice theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:msl}) and binary specialization
(Corollary~\ref{cor:binary}) in Section~\ref{sec:meet}; (4) a self-contained proof
of Dung's Fundamental Lemma and unconditional existence of a complete extension
(Section~\ref{sec:exist}); and (5) an order-theoretic re-derivation of the
grounded extension as the least complete extension, with uniqueness
(Section~\ref{sec:grounded}).

\section{Definitions}\label{sec:defs}

Fix a set $A$ and a relation $R : A \times A \to \mathrm{Prop}$; $R\,a\,b$ means
$a$ attacks $b$. Subsets of $A$ are ordered by inclusion.

\begin{definition}[Conflict-free]
A set $S \subseteq A$ is \emph{conflict-free} if no member of $S$ attacks another
member of $S$: $\forall a \in S,\ \forall b \in S,\ \neg\, R\,a\,b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Defense]
A set $S$ \emph{defends} an argument $a$, written $\Defends(S,a)$, if every
attacker of $a$ is counterattacked from within $S$:
$\forall b,\ R\,b\,a \Rightarrow \exists c \in S,\ R\,c\,b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissible]
A set $S$ is \emph{admissible} if it is conflict-free and defends each of its
members: $\Admissible(S) \iff \ConflictFree(S) \wedge (\forall a \in S,\ \Defends(S,a))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Characteristic operator]
The \emph{characteristic operator} $\Fop : \mathcal{P}(A) \to \mathcal{P}(A)$ is
$\Fop(S) = \{\, a \in A : \Defends(S,a) \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complete extension]
A set $S$ is a \emph{complete extension} if it is admissible and closed under
defense: $\Complete(S) \iff \Admissible(S) \wedge \Fop(S) \subseteq S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $S \subseteq T$ and $\Defends(S,a)$ then $\Defends(T,a)$; hence $\Fop$ is
monotone: $S \subseteq T \Rightarrow \Fop(S) \subseteq \Fop(T)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $b$ attacks $a$, some $c \in S$ attacks $b$; since $S \subseteq T$, that $c$
lies in $T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Downward closure of conflict-freedom]\label{lem:cfdown}
If $S \subseteq T$ and $T$ is conflict-free, then $S$ is conflict-free.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Restrict the quantifiers to the smaller set.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Complete extensions are fixed points]\label{lem:fixed}
If $S$ is a complete extension then $\Fop(S) = S$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Completeness gives $\Fop(S) \subseteq S$; admissibility gives $S \subseteq \Fop(S)$
(each member of $S$ is defended by $S$). Antisymmetry yields equality.
\end{proof}

Thus a complete extension is precisely a conflict-free fixed point of the monotone
operator $\Fop$.

\section{The Decisive Lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$\Fop$ stabilizes intersections]\label{thm:decisive}
Let $\Sfam$ be a family of complete extensions and $I = \bigcap \Sfam$. Then
$\Fop(I) \subseteq I$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $a \in \Fop(I)$ and let $E \in \Sfam$ be arbitrary. Since $I \subseteq E$,
Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} gives $\Fop(I) \subseteq \Fop(E)$, so $a \in \Fop(E)$. But
$E$ is complete, so $\Fop(E) = E$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:fixed}, whence $a \in E$. As
$E$ was arbitrary, $a \in \bigcap \Sfam = I$.
\end{proof}

Thus $\Fop$ restricts to a monotone self-map of the interval $[\varnothing, I]$,
and by Knaster--Tarski has a greatest fixed point there. We build it explicitly.

\section{The Meet of a Family of Complete Extensions}\label{sec:meet}

\begin{definition}[Family meet]
For a family $\Sfam$ of sets, define
\[
M(\Sfam) = \bigcup \big\{\, S : S \subseteq \textstyle\bigcap \Sfam \ \text{and}\ S \subseteq \Fop(S) \,\big\}:
\]
the union of all post-fixed points of $\Fop$ lying below the intersection.
\end{definition}

Throughout this section $\Sfam$ is a family of complete extensions.

\begin{lemma}[Below the intersection]\label{lem:below}
$M(\Sfam) \subseteq \bigcap \Sfam$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Any $x \in M(\Sfam)$ lies in some $S \subseteq \bigcap\Sfam$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Post-fixed]\label{lem:post}
$M(\Sfam) \subseteq \Fop(M(\Sfam))$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $x \in M(\Sfam)$, witnessed by $S$ with $S \subseteq \bigcap\Sfam$,
$S \subseteq \Fop(S)$, $x \in S$. Then $S \subseteq M(\Sfam)$, so
$\Fop(S) \subseteq \Fop(M(\Sfam))$ by monotonicity; since $x \in S \subseteq \Fop(S)$,
we get $x \in \Fop(M(\Sfam))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Pre-fixed]\label{lem:pre}
$\Fop(M(\Sfam)) \subseteq M(\Sfam)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We show $\Fop(M(\Sfam))$ belongs to the union defining $M(\Sfam)$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:below} and monotonicity, $\Fop(M(\Sfam)) \subseteq \Fop(\bigcap\Sfam) \subseteq \bigcap\Sfam$
using Theorem~\ref{thm:decisive}. Applying $\Fop$ to Lemma~\ref{lem:post} gives
$\Fop(M(\Sfam)) \subseteq \Fop(\Fop(M(\Sfam)))$, so it is a post-fixed point below
the intersection, hence contained in $M(\Sfam)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed point]\label{thm:meetfixed}
$\Fop(M(\Sfam)) = M(\Sfam)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Antisymmetry from Lemmas~\ref{lem:post} and \ref{lem:pre}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Conflict-freedom]\label{lem:meetcf}
If $\Sfam$ is nonempty, $M(\Sfam)$ is conflict-free.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Pick $E \in \Sfam$; then $M(\Sfam) \subseteq \bigcap\Sfam \subseteq E$ and $E$ is
conflict-free, so apply Lemma~\ref{lem:cfdown}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The meet is complete]\label{thm:meetcomplete}
If $\Sfam$ is a nonempty family of complete extensions, $M(\Sfam)$ is a complete
extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Conflict-free by Lemma~\ref{lem:meetcf}; a fixed point of $\Fop$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:meetfixed}, which supplies both $M(\Sfam) \subseteq \Fop(M(\Sfam))$
(admissibility: each member is defended) and $\Fop(M(\Sfam)) \subseteq M(\Sfam)$
(closure under defense).
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Lower bound]\label{lem:lb}
For every $E \in \Sfam$, $M(\Sfam) \subseteq E$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$M(\Sfam) \subseteq \bigcap\Sfam \subseteq E$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Greatest lower bound]\label{thm:glb}
If $L$ is a complete extension with $L \subseteq E$ for all $E \in \Sfam$, then
$L \subseteq M(\Sfam)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$L \subseteq \bigcap\Sfam$, and $L \subseteq \Fop(L)$ by admissibility, so $L$ is a
post-fixed point below the intersection; hence $L \subseteq M(\Sfam)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Meet-semilattice]\label{thm:msl}
For any argumentation framework $(A,R)$, with no finiteness or well-foundedness
hypothesis, the complete extensions ordered by inclusion form a meet-semilattice:
every nonempty family $\Sfam$ of complete extensions has a greatest lower bound
$M(\Sfam)$, which is itself complete.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:meetcomplete}, \ref{thm:glb} and Lemma~\ref{lem:lb}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Binary meet]\label{cor:binary}
For complete extensions $S, T$, put $S \wedge T = M(\{S,T\})$. Then $S \wedge T$ is
complete, $S \wedge T \subseteq S$, $S \wedge T \subseteq T$, and every complete
$L \subseteq S, T$ satisfies $L \subseteq S \wedge T$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:msl} to the nonempty family $\{S,T\}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Existence of Complete Extensions}\label{sec:exist}

\begin{theorem}[Dung's Fundamental Lemma]\label{thm:fund}
If $S$ is admissible and defends $a$, then $S \cup \{a\}$ is admissible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $S' = S \cup \{a\}$. (i) No $c \in S$ attacks $a$: else $S$, defending $a$,
supplies $d \in S$ attacking $c$, breaking conflict-freedom of $S$. (ii) $a$
attacks no $c \in S$: else $S$, defending $c$, supplies $d \in S$ attacking $a$,
contradicting (i). (iii) $a$ does not attack itself: else $S$ contains an attacker
of $a$, contradicting (i). Conflict-freedom of $S'$ follows by cases using
(i)--(iii); and $S'$ defends its members since $S \subseteq S'$ defends $a$ (by
hypothesis and monotonicity) and each $c \in S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Chains of admissible sets]\label{lem:chain}
The union of a chain of admissible sets is admissible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $c$ be a chain, $U = \bigcup c$. Conflict-freedom: $a,b \in U$ lie in
$S_1, S_2 \in c$; by totality one contains the other, placing both in a single
conflict-free member. Defense: $a \in U$ lies in some $S \in c$ defending it, and
$S \subseteq U$, so $U$ defends $a$ by monotonicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]\label{thm:exist}
Every argumentation framework has a complete extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The empty set is admissible. By Lemma~\ref{lem:chain} every chain of admissible
sets has an admissible upper bound, so by Zorn's Lemma a maximal admissible set
$m$ exists. If $a \in \Fop(m)$, the Fundamental Lemma makes $m \cup \{a\}$
admissible; maximality forces $m \cup \{a\} = m$, so $a \in m$. Hence
$\Fop(m) \subseteq m$ and $m$ is complete.
\end{proof}

\section{The Grounded Extension as the Bottom of the Semilattice}\label{sec:grounded}

\begin{theorem}[Least complete extension]\label{thm:least}
Every argumentation framework has a least complete extension.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{C} = \{S : \Complete(S)\}$, nonempty by Theorem~\ref{thm:exist}. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:meetcomplete}, $M(\mathcal{C})$ is complete, and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:lb} it is contained in every member of $\mathcal{C}$, i.e. in every
complete extension.
\end{proof}

The least complete extension is Dung's \emph{grounded extension}. Whereas the
classical construction obtains it as the (in general transfinite) least fixed
point of $\Fop$ from the empty set, Theorem~\ref{thm:least} obtains it as the
bottom of the meet-semilattice, bypassing iteration.

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]\label{thm:unique}
If $L$ and $L'$ are both least complete extensions, then $L = L'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$L \subseteq L'$ (as $L$ is least, $L'$ complete) and $L' \subseteq L$; apply
antisymmetry.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

The proofs assume neither finiteness of the framework nor well-foundedness of the
attack relation. The only tools are monotonicity and the Knaster--Tarski union,
and the pivotal input is Theorem~\ref{thm:decisive}: the closure of intersections
under $\Fop$. This converts the search for a common lower bound into a
greatest-fixed-point problem on a fixed interval, solved uniformly. The naive
intersection $\bigcap\Sfam$ is conflict-free and $\Fop$-closed but need not defend
its own members; the meet $M(\Sfam)$ is its largest self-defending subset, the
exact correction that restores admissibility while preserving the
greatest-lower-bound property.

\section{Future Work}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Directed joins and the complete lattice.} Whether directed families
of complete extensions have joins, upgrading the meet-semilattice to a complete
semilattice; the chain-union machinery of Section~\ref{sec:exist} is the natural
tool.
\item \textbf{A dichotomy for stable existence.} A framework admits a stable
extension iff some preferred extension leaves no argument undecided; since
preferred extensions are the maximal complete extensions, this is a boundary
condition on the maximal elements of the semilattice.
\item \textbf{Topological reflection.} A poset with a least element has a
contractible order complex; the grounded extension is such a least element, so the
nerve of the poset of complete extensions is contractible.
\item \textbf{Semi-stable and ideal semantics.} The ideal extension---the greatest
admissible set contained in every preferred extension---should arise as a fixed
point reachable by monotone iteration, inheriting existence from the chain-union
lemma.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The complete extensions of an arbitrary argumentation framework form a
meet-semilattice, via an explicit construction driven by the observation that the
defense operator stabilizes intersections of complete extensions. Feeding the
family of all complete extensions into the meet recovers the grounded extension as
a unique bottom element, giving an iteration-free proof of its existence and
uniqueness, with no hypothesis on the size of the framework or the shape of its
attack relation.

